WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.040
A character's outfit helps define who the
character is in the context of the film — particularly

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:08.120
when it comes to period pieces and superhero
movies.

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:12.210
Seemingly tiny incorrect details can cause
a huge uproar among fans.

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:16.500
From metal bikinis to sparkly hot pants, let's
take a look at some of the most controversial

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:19.300
movie costumes — and the explanations behind
them.

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:21.600
Karen Gillan in Jumanji

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:27.700
In September 2016, audiences got their first
look at a set photo from Jake Kasdan's Jumanji:

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:32.930
Welcome to the Jungle, a new adventure inspired
by the beloved 1995 Robin Williams film.

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:38.140
In the photo, Dwayne Johnson, Kevin Hart,
and Jack Black are each wearing jungle-appropriate

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:42.520
outfits…alongside co-star Karen Gillan,
who's wearing what amounts to child-sized

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:43.020
clothing.

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:48.580
The photo prompted immediate criticism, prompting
Gillan to try and defuse controversy by telling

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:51.890
The Hollywood Reporter, "The payoff is worth
it, I promise!"

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:56.040
The Hollywood Reporter later revealed that
the first footage for the film does explain

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:57.480
Gillan's skimpy outfit.

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:00.960
"The plot involves four high-school students
who are forced to clean out the basement of

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:02.650
their school while in detention.

00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:06.240
They find an old video game (rather than a
board game) and each chooses a character to

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:06.740
play."

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:10.940
So is the costume meant as tongue-in-cheek
criticism of how video games portray women?

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:11.680
Maybe!

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:13.040
We'll have to wait and see.

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:15.760
George Clooney in Batman &amp; Robin

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:20.140
Joel Schumacher's Batman &amp; Robin was a disaster,
to put it mildly.

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:24.750
Setting aside the cartoony elements, the terrible
dialogue, and poor plot choices, fans will

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:27.880
never forget the costumes — specifically
the Bat-nipples.

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:29.520
"You can milk anything with nipples"

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:32.119
Schumacher explained his reasoning in a Batman
special,

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:36.040
"The bodies for the suits — the inspiration
for them are Greek statues that have perfect

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:36.540
bodies.

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:40.560
And, so, we're molding this perfect body in
rubber, and they're anatomically erotic.

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:44.960
So, it never occurred to me not to put nipples
on the men's suits because I didn't know the

00:01:44.969 --> 00:01:47.490
male nipple was a controversial body part."

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:49.380
Ryan Reynolds in Green Lantern

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:54.130
Oscar winning costume designer Ngila Dickson
came up with the unique idea that Green Lantern's

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:56.760
costume should be a manifestation of his power.

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:00.899
That could have led to some pretty cool visuals
— but as fans immediately realized when

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:04.720
the first images of Green Lantern were released,
the special effects just weren't up to the

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:05.220
job.

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:08.800
"Woah green!"

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:12.880
Wow

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:14.380
"I know, right?!"

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:18.560
Even star Ryan Reynolds also complained about
how the costume made his job much harder to

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:19.560
do, saying,

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:23.069
"It's brutal doing a film where you're wearing
a motion capture suit for the whole time.

00:02:23.069 --> 00:02:26.180
I didn't even get to see the Green Lantern
suit until the first trailer.

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:27.860
I never even knew what it looked like."

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:30.420
Rebel Wilson in Pitch Perfect 3

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:36.220
Even before Pitch Perfect 3 finished filming,
the production caught flak after Rebel Wilson

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:41.079
posted a photo online of herself alongside
co-stars Brittany Snow and Chrissie Fit, causing

00:02:41.079 --> 00:02:46.269
fans online to accuse the film of size discrimination
because Wilson's outfit had sleeves, while

00:02:46.269 --> 00:02:48.299
her costars had bare arms.

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:48.800
That's right.

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:51.620
This was the start...of "sleevegate."

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:55.980
Costume designer Salvador Perez ultimately
took to Twitter to try and put out the fire.

00:02:55.989 --> 00:02:59.799
"I let each actor decide how their costume
fit, it was their choice.

00:02:59.799 --> 00:03:02.030
Rebel, Ester &amp; Hana Mae wanted sleeves."

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:06.730
In another photo posted by Kelley Jakle, each
of the stars are seen wearing a variety of

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:08.790
styles of the same sailor top.

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:12.390
It appears that the photo that ignited the
controversy in the first place may have been

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:13.390
taken out of context.

00:03:13.390 --> 00:03:16.549
And that was the end...of "sleevegate."

00:03:16.549 --> 00:03:19.150
Margot Robbie in Suicide Squad

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:23.640
Harley Quinn's big-screen debut in David Ayer's
Suicide Squad was one of the movie's biggest

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:27.439
draws, especially since Margot Robbie was
playing the famed villain.

00:03:27.439 --> 00:03:31.740
As with any other comic book character, a
lot of media attention focused on her costume

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:34.019
— particularly how much of it was missing.

00:03:34.019 --> 00:03:37.980
When asked about her body-hugging costume,
Robbie told The New York Times that Harley

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:41.140
was, quote, "wearing hot pants because they're
sparkly and fun."

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:44.150
But the actress personally felt self-conscious
about the outfit.

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:48.760
Ayer defended the look by saying that he "didn't
think denim overalls would be appropriate

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:50.040
for that character."

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:52.300
Because that clearly was the only other possible
choice.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:55.400
Ryan Reynolds in X-Men Origins: Wolverine

00:03:55.720 --> 00:04:00.260
You may have blocked it from your memory,
but Ryan Reynolds played Deadpool before Deadpool.

00:04:00.269 --> 00:04:04.219
Unfortunately, during his first outing as
the character in X-Men Origins: Wolverine,

00:04:04.219 --> 00:04:08.159
the filmmakers decided to make the brave,
bold choice of doing everything they could

00:04:08.159 --> 00:04:09.890
to insult the character's fans.

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:14.159
They took away his comic book outfit, including
his iconic mask, and they sewed his mouth

00:04:14.159 --> 00:04:18.549
shut — leaving a character who's literally
referred to as "the merc with a mouth" physically

00:04:18.549 --> 00:04:19.660
unable to speak.

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:24.000
Studio ADI, who applied the creature effects
for the film, made a video explaining their

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:25.610
whole side of the Deadpool tale.

00:04:25.610 --> 00:04:29.919
The artists believe the studio didn't intend
for Deadpool to be fully formed in Wolverine,

00:04:29.919 --> 00:04:32.650
just a setup for what he could become in a
later film.

00:04:32.650 --> 00:04:34.140
"This is sort of a pre-Deadpool.

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:38.140
If you think of this as an embryonic Deadpool
it might make more sense."

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:41.180
Bryce Dallas Howard in Jurassic World

00:04:41.199 --> 00:04:45.580
Though audiences generally loved the big,
dino-action, there was one big problem with

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:50.370
Jurassic World for some fans: Bryce Dallas
Howard's heels, which somehow stay magically

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:55.120
glued to her feet even when running from a
T-rex through mud, rain, and wet concrete.

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:57.280
But Howard defended the choice, telling Yahoo!

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:00.210
News, "I'm better equipped to run when I have
shoes on my feet.

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:01.530
So that's my perspective on it.

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:03.580
I don't think she would carry around flats
with her.

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:07.410
I think she's somebody who could sprint a
marathon in heels…For me, it was actually

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:11.350
logical for her to be in that very illogical
situation because she doesn't belong in the

00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:14.889
jungle and yet she finds herself there and
has to adapt."

00:05:14.889 --> 00:05:17.080
Sylvester Stallone in Judge Dredd

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:21.699
In the comics, Judge Dredd famously never
reveals his true face, a decision that's symbolic

00:05:21.699 --> 00:05:23.400
of his dedication to his job.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:25.410
There is no other, secret identity.

00:05:25.410 --> 00:05:27.410
His helmet isn't a mask — it's who he is.

00:05:27.410 --> 00:05:31.940
So it was a huge problem for fans when star
Sylvester Stallone took his helmet off less

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:33.690
than 20 minutes into the film.

00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:37.750
The reason was simple: Stallone's celebrity
was more important than the source material,

00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:40.150
and he wanted to showcase that famous face.

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:44.620
After all, what's the point in signing a superstar
to a role when you never get to even see him?

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:48.842
As Judge Dredd writer Alan Grant said, "The
story was subservient to Stallone, who, I

00:05:48.842 --> 00:05:50.930
think, takes himself too seriously."

00:05:50.930 --> 00:05:51.900
Or in other words:

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:54.180
"I am the law!"

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:56.980
Carrie Fisher in Return of the Jedi

00:05:57.300 --> 00:06:01.660
Princess Leia's metal bikini struck an immediate
chord with adolescent male fans of the Star

00:06:01.660 --> 00:06:04.640
Wars series from the moment it debuted in
Return of the Jedi.

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:09.379
But while the outfit was based on Frank Frazetta's
legendary painting The Princess of Mars, plenty

00:06:09.379 --> 00:06:14.610
of critics have since found the "slave Leia"
costume to be a prime example of sexual objectification

00:06:14.610 --> 00:06:18.940
— a sexist treatment of the series' only
notable female character at the time.

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:22.710
Carrie Fisher, who played Leia and had to
put up with decades of discussion around the

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:27.199
outfit, warned Daisy Ridley, star of The Force
Awakens, "Don't be a slave like I was…

00:06:27.199 --> 00:06:29.220
You keep fighting against that slave outfit."

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:33.879
Fisher later tweeted, "That chain only 'enslaved'
me until I could use the frabjous thing to

00:06:33.879 --> 00:06:37.800
KILL THAT DROOLING SWOLLEN SUPERTONGUED SLUG
&amp; whirl him off into infinity."

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:40.840
As always, Fisher had the last — and best
— word.

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:41.979
Thanks for watching!

00:06:41.979 --> 00:06:44.820
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:47.190
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

